## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end multi-tenant design, runtime feature toggles, schema evolution, and deterministic replay using AWS DVA primitives.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant isolation with Lake Formation and tenant-scoped keys
- Schema evolution via Glue Schema Registry with backward/forward compatibility
- Deterministic replay using per-tenant replay token in DynamoDB
- Idempotent writes by deduping event_id in DynamoDB
- End-to-end observability with OpenTelemetry; secure access controls

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo dedupe and write path
async function processEvent(evt){
  const {tenant_id, event_id, payload} = evt;
  // ensure idempotent dedupe
  const exists = await dynamo.get({TableName:'TenantDedup', Key:{tenant_id, event_id}});
  if (exists.Item) return;
  await dynamo.put({TableName:'TenantDedup', Item:{tenant_id, event_id, ts: Date.now()}});
  // write raw to S3 with partition tenant/date (implementation omitted)
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle a schema-breaking change in a live tenant dataset?
- How would you validate replay correctness under a backfill window?

## Why This Is Asked
This question introduces Glue Schema Registry and schema evolution, a common but underrepresented real-world constraint for beginner engineers. It tests practical streaming ingestion, data validation, and basic tracing.

## Key Concepts
- Glue Schema Registry
- Backward-compatible schema
- Data enrichment from DynamoDB
- Parquet on S3 with date partition
- Kinesis Data Streams
- DLQ and observability (X-Ray)
- LocalStack testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for the consumer path
async function handleRecord(record){
  const valid = validateWithSchema(registry='Telemetry', version='latest', record);
  if(valid){
    const meta = await dynamo.getItem({deviceId: record.deviceId});
    const enriched = {...record, meta};
    await s3.putParquet(enriched, partitionBy='date');
  } else {
    await dlq.push(record);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle schema evolution with multiple producers using backward compatibility?
- How would you test end-to-end locally when Glue Schema Registry is not available?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design governance, data isolation, and cost visibility in a scalable multi-tenant pipeline. It requires concrete use of DVA primitives and cross-region considerations.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant quotas and backpressure
- Tenant-specific masking rules and privacy controls
- Cost attribution via tagging and per-tenant accounting
- Cross-region data movement and observability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: quota check at ingest
function checkQuota(tenantId){
  const {limit, consumed} = quotas[tenantId];
  if(consumed >= limit) throw new BackpressureError('Quota exceeded');
  quotas[tenantId].consumed += 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test quota hardening and masking correctness? 
- How would you handle tenant onboarding/offboarding without data gaps?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests capacity planning and backpressure design in real-world telemetry pipelines at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant quotas and shard allocation strategy
- Upstream backpressure signaling (HTTP 429/Retry-After)
- Lambda vs. Fargate processing roles
- Durable S3 storage with lifecycle policies
- DynamoDB-backed budget enforcement
- Comprehensive observability and failure handling

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo quota check (conceptual)
function canIngest(tenantId, amount) {
  const budget = dynamo.get(tenantId);
  if (budget.remaining < amount) return false;
}

